# From Symmetry to Selected Order

Begin with the central paradox: a cold sample may look magnetized, yet the signed mean of every finite zero-field symmetric ensemble is exactly zero. Use the interactive phase mixer to see how two ordered phases cancel without either phase being disordered.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

[Learn more about the Ising model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ising_model) and then run the direct finite-state calculation.

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite-volume cancellation proof</summary>
Let $F$ be an involution, with $E(F\omega)=E(\omega)$ and $M(F\omega)=-M(\omega)$. Reindexing the finite numerator by $F$ makes it equal to its own negative, so it is zero. The partition function is a nonempty sum of positive exponentials, hence division preserves zero.
</details>

## Compressing an exponential sum

The transfer matrix turns a sum over $2^n$ periodic-chain configurations into two scalar powers. Compare enumeration with the closed formula and inspect the two eigenvalues.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the spectral decomposition</summary>
The vectors $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ diagonalize the matrix, with eigenvalues $2\cosh eta$ and $2\sinh eta$. Therefore the trace of the $n$th power is the sum of their $n$th powers.
</details>

## The distinguished self-dual point

Explore the unique positive solution of $\sinh(2 eta)=1$ and watch the residual vanish near $ eta_c=\log(1+\sqrt2)/2$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The corresponding temperature is $T_c=1/ eta_c$, between $2$ and $3$. [Read about Kramers–Wannier duality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kramers%E2%80%93Wannier_duality).

## Domain walls and low-temperature order

